Found it. This is a known, still-unfixed bug in this codebase — `LESSONS.md:7` documents it as upstream issue #88, but the code still has it.

## Root cause: the password hash hook only runs on INSERT

`src/user/user.entity.ts:28`
```ts
@BeforeInsert()
async hashPassword() {
  this.password = await argon2.hash(this.password);
}
```

`@BeforeInsert()` fires on registration and **never again**. There is no `@BeforeUpdate()`.

## How plaintext gets in

`src/user/user.service.ts:74`
```ts
async update(id: number, dto: UpdateUserDto) {
  let toUpdate = await this.userRepository.findOne(id);
  delete toUpdate.password;          // drops the *good* argon2 hash
  let updated = Object.assign(toUpdate, dto);   // copies raw body.password back in
  return await this.userRepository.save(updated);  // save() → UPDATE → hook skipped
}
```

`UpdateUserDto` (`src/user/dto/update-user.dto.ts`) declares no `password` field — but a DTO class is a compile-time annotation, it strips nothing at runtime. And unlike `create` and `login`, the `PUT /user` handler has **no `ValidationPipe`** (`src/user/user.controller.ts:26` vs `:31` and `:42`). So `{"user":{"password":"hunter2"}}` sails through and `hunter2` lands in the `password` column verbatim.

## Why login then fails

`src/user/user.service.ts:31`
```ts
if (await argon2.verify(user.password, password)) { return user; }
```

`user.password` is now `hunter2`, not an argon2 hash. `argon2.verify` can't match it — depending on the argon2 version it either returns false or throws on the malformed hash string, so the user sees a 401 (`{errors: {User: ' not found'}}`) or a 500. Either way they're locked out, and there's no password-reset route to recover through.

## Two things to flag beyond the lockout

1. **Every changed password is sitting in MySQL in plaintext.** That's a disclosure incident, not just a bug. Those credentials should be treated as compromised.
2. Because the plaintext is readable, affected users are recoverable — you can re-hash the existing column value in place and their new password starts working. Worth doing as a migration alongside the code fix.

## The fix

Add a `@BeforeUpdate()` sibling that only hashes when the value isn't already a hash, and stop merging the raw body:

```ts
// user.entity.ts
@BeforeInsert()
@BeforeUpdate()
async hashPassword() {
  if (this.password && !this.password.startsWith('$argon2')) {
    this.password = await argon2.hash(this.password);
  }
}
```

```ts
// user.service.ts — copy allowed fields by name instead of Object.assign
const { username, email, bio, image } = dto;
```
...plus `@UsePipes(new ValidationPipe({whitelist: true, forbidNonWhitelisted: true}))` on `PUT /user`, and a separate deliberate `changePassword` path if you actually want users to be able to change it (right now the only way is this accidental one).

Want me to apply the fix, and write the re-hash migration for the already-affected rows?

